### 5.2 ActionList Configuration
After successful demo configuration, you MUST check if there are create components in the same directory level and add them to the actionList prop.

**Check for related components**:
1. **Create Component Check**: Look for `src/[module]/create/` directory  
   - If create component exists, add it to actionList

**ActionList Implementation**:
When create components are found in the same module directory, you MUST update the list component's view.js file to include them in the actionList prop:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list/view.js
const View = createVisualComponent({
  // ... existing props
  actionList: [
    // Add create component if it exists  
    ...(Create ? [{ component: <Create baseUri={baseUri} onSuccess={() => [module]DataList.handlerMap.load()} /> }] : [])
  ],
  // ... rest of component
});
```

**Required imports**:
Add the necessary imports at the top of the view.js file:
```javascript
// Add this import if the create component exists
import Create from "../create/create.js";
```

**Purpose**: This ensures that list components automatically include related create components as actions, providing a complete interface when create components are present in the same module directory.

### 5.3 Remove Default Values
After configuring the actionList, you MUST remove the default values for filterList and sorterList in the list component.

**Remove Default Values**:
Update the list component's defaultProps to remove the placeholder filterList and sorterList values:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list.js
defaultProps: {
  ...View.defaultProps,
  // Remove these default values:
  // filterList: [{ key: "yourFilterItem", value: true }],
  // sorterList: [{ key: "yourSorterItem", ascending: true }],
},
```

**Purpose**: This removes placeholder default values that were generated by uu5Generate, allowing the component to work with empty filter and sorter lists by default.

## 6. Demo Configuration

### 6.1 Display Value Guidelines
After removing default values, you MUST ensure proper display of values in list components.

**Display Guidelines**:
1. **Exclude system attributes**: Do NOT display sysCts, sysMts, sysRev, or other system-generated fields in seriesList
2. **Show only relevant data**: Display only business-relevant fields that users need to see
3. **Proper data formatting**: Format dates, numbers, and other data types appropriately in dataItem functions
4. **Handle null/undefined values**: Gracefully handle missing or empty data in dataItem functions

**Example seriesList (excluding system attributes)**:
```javascript
function getSerieList(viewLsi) {
  return [
    {
      value: "field1",
      label: viewLsi.field1,
      dataItem: ({ data: entityDto }) => entityDto.data.field1,
    },
    {
      value: "field2", 
      label: viewLsi.field2,
      dataItem: ({ data: entityDto }) => entityDto.data.field2,
    },
    // Do NOT include sysCts, sysMts, sysRev, etc.
  ];
}
```

### 6.2 Detail Action Integration
After removing default values, you MUST add detail actions to each row of the list component if a detail component exists in the same module directory.

**Check for Detail Component**:
1. **Detail Component Check**: Look for `src/[module]/detail/` directory or `src/[module]/detail.js` file
   - If detail component exists, add it to item actions

**Detail Action Implementation**:
When detail components are found in the same module directory, you MUST update the list component's view.js file to include detail actions:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list/view.js
import Detail from "../detail.js";
import { useModal } from "uu5g05-elements";

// In the render function
const [detailModalProps, openDetailModal, closeDetailModal] = useModal();

// Update getItemActions function
function getItemActions({ [Module]Dto }, baseUri, openDetailModal) {
  const actionList = [
    {
      icon: "uugds-open-in-modal",
      colorScheme: "blue",
      tooltip: "View Details",
      onClick: (e) => {
        openDetailModal({
          data: {
            baseUri,
            [identifier]: [Module]Dto.data.[identifier]
          }
        });
      },
      collapsed: false,
    },
  ];
  return actionList;
}

// Update handleGetItemActionList
const handleGetItemActionList = ({ data: [Module]Dto }) => {
  return getItemActions({ [Module]Dto }, baseUri, openDetailModal);
};

// Add modal rendering
{detailModalProps.open && (
  <Detail
    {...detailModalProps}
    baseUri={detailModalProps.data?.baseUri}
    [identifier]={detailModalProps.data?.[identifier]}
    displayAsModal={true}
    onClose={closeDetailModal}
  />
)}
```

**Required imports**:
Add the necessary imports at the top of the view.js file:
```javascript
import Detail from "../detail.js";
import { useModal } from "uu5g05-elements";
```

**Purpose**: This ensures that list components automatically include detail actions when detail components are present, providing a complete interface for viewing individual entity details.

### 6.3 State Badge Rendering
After removing default values, you MUST implement Badge rendering for state fields in list components.

**State Badge Implementation**:
When state fields are present in the list component, you MUST update the content.js file to render state values as Badges:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list/content.js
import { Badge } from "uu5g05-elements";

// Add toCamelCase method to Content component
toCamelCase(str) {
  if (!str) return str;
  return str
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[-_\s]+(.)?/g, (_, char) => (char ? char.toUpperCase() : ''))
    .replace(/^(.)/, (char) => char.toUpperCase());
},

// Add getStateColorScheme method to Content component
getStateColorScheme(state) {
  switch (state?.toLowerCase()) {
    case "active":
    case "running":
      return "green";
    case "inactive":
    case "stopped":
      return "red";
    case "pending":
    case "waiting":
      return "orange";
    case "completed":
    case "finished":
      return "blue";
    default:
      return "cyan";
  }
},

// Update columnList to use custom StateCellWrapper for state column
const columnList = useMemo(() => {
  const CellWrapper = (cellProps) => <Cell {...cellProps} onClick={onItemClick} />;
  
  const StateCellWrapper = (cellProps) => {
    const { data } = cellProps;
    const stateValue = data?.data?.state;
    
    return (
      <Cell {...cellProps} onClick={onItemClick}>
        {stateValue && (
          <Badge colorScheme={Content.getStateColorScheme(stateValue)} size="xl">
            {Content.toCamelCase(stateValue)}
          </Badge>
        )}
      </Cell>
    );
  };

  return serieList.map((serie) => {
    const cellComponent = serie.value === "state" ? StateCellWrapper : CellWrapper;
    return { value: serie.value, cellComponent };
  });
}, [onItemClick, serieList]);
```

**Purpose**: This ensures that state fields in list components are rendered as prominent, color-coded badges with proper CamelCase formatting for better user experience and visual clarity.

### 6.4 Date Alignment
After removing default values, you MUST align date and datetime fields to the right in the list component.

**Date Alignment Implementation**:
When date or datetime fields are present in the list component, you MUST add alignment properties and use DateTime component in the getSerieList function:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list/view.js
import { DateTime } from "uu5g05-elements";

function getSerieList(viewLsi) {
  return [
    // ... other fields
    {
      value: "dateField",
      label: viewLsi.dateField,
      dataItem: ({ data: entityDto }) => entityDto.data.dateField ? (
        <DateTime value={entityDto.data.dateField} dateFormat="short" timeFormat="none" />
      ) : null,
      horizontalAlignment: "right",
    },
    {
      value: "datetimeField", 
      label: viewLsi.datetimeField,
      dataItem: ({ data: entityDto }) => entityDto.data.datetimeField ? (
        <DateTime value={entityDto.data.datetimeField} dateFormat="short" timeFormat="none" />
      ) : null,
      horizontalAlignment: "right",
    },
    // ... more fields
  ];
}
```

**Purpose**: This ensures that date and datetime fields are right-aligned in the table for better readability and professional appearance, following standard table formatting conventions.

### 6.5 Title and Subtitle Configuration
After removing default values, you MUST configure proper title and subtitle for the list component.

**Title and Subtitle Implementation**:
Update the ContentContainer.splitProps configuration in the view.js file to set meaningful titles and subtitles:

```javascript
// In src/[module]/list/view.js
const { containerProps } = ContentContainer.splitProps(propsToPass, {
  title: "[Module] list",  // e.g., "Auction list", "Note list", "Library list"
